{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nExisting stores — including client deployments — have no `.bridge_state/projects.json` and no\n`bridge_project` on any ticket. Without a migration they read every ticket as absent-project with no\nrecorded legacy default for the resolver to fall back to, so a store that syncs today stops syncing on\nupgrade.\n\n## Prerequisites (substrate, from cited deps — already `depends_on` edges)\n\n- `[rebar:c927-0a68-4465-42a2]` — the projects-mapping store `projects_store.py`: the\n  `.bridge_state/projects.json` record (`{version, legacy_default, projects}`), `load_mapping`, the\n  `Mapping` dataclass, the tri-state resolver `resolve_project` (absent field → `legacy_default`;\n  `\"\"` → `None`; key → verbatim), and `set_project`/`read_projects`.\n- `[rebar:67f9-4a07-9667-446a]` — registers `multi-project-bridge` in `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES` (`compat.py:63`), so\n  a store may stamp it and still pass its own compat gate.\n\n`Backend.project` (`_backend.py:598-605`, the effective write/create scope) is pre-existing.\n\n## Approach\n\nShip the migration as ensure units on the idempotent registry (`src/rebar/_store/ensures.py`,\n`REGISTRY_IDS` `:66`, `_registry()` `:82`) — rebar's sanctioned seam (`docs/migrations.md:40-56`; the\nordered School-A ledger is deliberately not built, `:14-16`). Units run at init/re-init,\n`fsck --repair`, and MCP boot (`:57-65`), so a client store converges with no operator action.\n\n**No ticket events.** An EDIT-per-ticket migration would be a four-figure event write under the store\nlock, and MCP-boot runs the sweep on a short lock budget and skips when contended (`:63-65`) — so it\nwould silently skip at one entry point and converge unpredictably. Instead the unit records\n`legacy_default` once, and `resolve_project` `[rebar:c927-0a68-4465-42a2]` treats every absent-field ticket as\nbelonging to it. One small write migrates the whole store.\n\nTwo units, both check-then-act and safe to re-run:\n\n1. **Seed the mapping.** If `.bridge_state/projects.json` `[rebar:c927-0a68-4465-42a2]` is absent, write it with\n   `legacy_default` and a single `projects` entry from `Backend.project` (`_backend.py:598-605`); if\n   present, return `ok` and write nothing. Tree-check the committed\n   `tickets:.bridge_state/projects.json` blob before committing so a converged store makes zero commits\n   (`docs/migrations.md:45-46`).\n2. **Stamp the capability (idempotent convergence).** Only when the mapping holds more than one\n   project, add `multi-project-bridge` to `.store-compat.json`'s `required_capabilities`; a\n   single-project store is never stamped and stays usable by older binaries. This level-triggered\n   ensure unit is decoupled from *how* the store became multi-project: the seed above only ever makes a\n   **one-project** mapping, so this unit never fires from the same sweep's seed — it fires on a later\n   sweep once an operator added a second project (via `set_project` `[rebar:c927-0a68-4465-42a2]` or a direct\n   edit). No sibling owns a write-time stamp (7032 only *documents* the token), so this ensure unit is\n   the single authoritative stamp: a multi-project store cannot be left unstamped because the next\n   init/`fsck`/MCP-boot sweep converges it. The token is already in `KNOWN_CAPABILITIES`\n   (`compat.py:63`) `[rebar:67f9-4a07-9667-446a]`, so a stamped store still passes `check_store_compat` on this\n   binary — this story makes **no** `compat.py` edit, and a test asserts a two-project stamped store\n   passes the gate.\n\nIds are new and never reused (`docs/migrations.md:50-51`).\n\n**Unit bodies live in a new `src/rebar/_store/project_ensures.py`**, not `init.py` (where the six\nexisting units live, e.g. `_store_compat_unit` `:509`). `init.py` is **758 lines** — ~42 under the\n800-line gate (`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), so this is a **cohesion** choice, not size-forced:\nthe projects migration is distinct from init's `.gitignore`/`.gitattributes` constants. `ensures.py`\n(257 lines) gains only its two registry entries.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/project_ensures.py` (new) — both unit bodies\n- `src/rebar/_store/ensures.py` — two `REGISTRY_IDS` entries + two `_registry()` mappings\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_ensures.py` — update the hardcoded `expected` id-set literal in\n  `test_registry_id_set_is_frozen` (`:57`, the six existing ids) to add the two new ids so the\n  registry-agreement test passes\n- `docs/migrations.md` — document the two units and the `multi-project-bridge` token's meaning for\n  other binaries/operators (it gates `.store-compat.json` compatibility)\n- `tests/integration/test_ensure_project_mapping.py` (new) — the behavioural oracle below\n\n## Testing\n\nReal temp git repos and refs (the shared standard's git row) — the units commit to `tickets` and the\nzero-commit-when-converged property is only observable against real refs.\n\n- Happy path: sweeping a no-mapping store creates one whose `legacy_default` equals the backend\n  project, and every ticket resolves to it via `resolve_project`.\n- Load-bearing: the sweep writes **zero ticket events** — assert the event-file set is identical before\n  and after (distinguishes this from an EDIT-per-ticket migration).\n- Idempotence: a second sweep returns `ok` for both units and the tickets-branch tip SHA is unchanged.\n- Conditional stamp (contrast pair): one-project → `required_capabilities` empty; two-project → exactly\n  `multi-project-bridge`. The one-project half proves the stamp is genuinely conditional.\n- Compat-gate safety: a two-project stamped store still passes `check_store_compat` on this binary.\n- Failure path: a raising unit is recorded `failed` and does not abort the sweep or caller.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A no-mapping store gains one whose `legacy_default` equals the configured backend project;\n      real-git test reads the seeded record.\n- [ ] Every absent-`bridge_project` ticket resolves to that default via `resolve_project`\n      `[rebar:c927-0a68-4465-42a2]`; same test.\n- [ ] The sweep writes zero ticket events; the event-file set is identical before and after.\n- [ ] A second sweep returns `ok` for both units.\n- [ ] A second sweep leaves the tickets-branch tip SHA unchanged (compared across two sweeps).\n- [ ] A one-project mapping leaves `required_capabilities` empty; real-git test.\n- [ ] A two-project mapping adds exactly `multi-project-bridge`; real-git test.\n- [ ] A two-project stamped store still passes `check_store_compat` on this binary; test invokes the\n      gate.\n- [ ] The two new ids are added to `REGISTRY_IDS`.\n- [ ] The two new ids are mapped in `_registry()`.\n- [ ] The two new ids are added to the `expected` literal in `test_registry_id_set_is_frozen`\n      (`test_ensures.py:57`) so that test passes.\n- [ ] A raising unit is recorded `failed` and does not abort the sweep or caller; test monkeypatches a\n      raising unit into `_registry()`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786665655580155001,"uuid":"a64af345-e181-49a2-95d5-5d56ca6b775d"}